course: myths
lesson: 26
title: Sleepovers and spooky stories
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Torch under the chin, someone whispers: "Say Bloody Mary three times in the mirror in the dark and she appears!" Everyone shrieks and nobody wants to go to the bathroom alone.
explain: Spooky stories are fun because your brain is a brilliant pattern-finder: in the dark it turns a coat into a figure, a creak into footsteps, and a tired face in a mirror into a stranger.
explain: Stare at your own face in dim light for a minute and it can seem to warp and change; that is your brain filling gaps, not a ghost, and scientists have studied it.
explain: Many sleepover tales, like the hook-handed man or the calls "from inside the house", are old urban legends told for decades and even made into films.
explain: Enjoy them as stories, and if someone gets truly scared, stop and remind them it's a game; some tales, like the lake monster, even hide a real lesson: deep water is dangerous.
fact: Staring at your face in a mirror in dim light can make it seem to warp or change into a stranger's; scientists have studied this effect, and it happens in your brain.
fact: The story of the man with a hook for a hand is an urban legend that has been told since at least the 1950s.
fact: Scratching and creaking at night usually come from mice, branches, wind, cooling pipes or a house settling (its wood and walls shifting a tiny bit) as it cools; turning on a light and looking, or telling an adult, usually solves the mystery.
fact: There is no good evidence that putting a sleeping person's hand in warm water makes them wet themselves, and pranks on sleeping friends are unkind.
fact: Lake-monster stories are often told to keep children away from water, and deep water really is dangerous: it can be cold, deep and full of weeds, so never swim without an adult.
fact: There is no evidence that ghosts exist or that "a ghost's breath" can haunt you.
fact: A glass or pointer on a spirit board moves because of tiny movements people make without knowing it, called the ideomotor effect.
fact: Black cats bringing bad luck is a superstition (a belief about luck or magic with no evidence); in some places, such as parts of Britain and Japan, they are even thought to bring good luck.
fact: The "calls coming from inside the house" story is an old urban legend that was turned into films.
fact: In the dark, our brains are more likely to imagine danger, which is why ordinary shapes and sounds feel scary at night.
fact: Spooky stories are fine as fun, but stop when someone is truly frightened.
exercise: Why might a face in a mirror in dim light seem to change? | Your brain fills in gaps and warps what it sees | A ghost is trying to take your place | Mirrors bend light more at night | The mirror is too old to show people's faces properly
exercise: How old is the hook-handed man story? | Told since at least the 1950s | It happened last year in your town | It comes from ancient Rome | It was invented by a film last year
exercise: What usually causes scratching and creaking at night? | Mice, branches, wind, pipes or the house settling | Ghosts trying to get into the room | Monsters living in the walls | Nothing at all, because sounds stop when it's night
exercise: Does a hand in warm water make a sleeping friend wet themselves? | There is no good evidence, and pranks on sleepers are unkind | Yes, it works on everyone every time | Yes, but only if the water is very hot | Only if the friend is younger than you
exercise: What real lesson is hidden in the lake-monster story? | Deep water is dangerous, so never swim without an adult | Lakes are always perfectly safe if no monster lives in them | Monsters only come out on Sundays | Only very deep lakes have water in them
exercise: Can a ghost's breath in a graveyard haunt you? | No, there is no evidence ghosts exist | Yes, if you breathe in at midnight | Yes, but only in very old graveyards | Only if you hold your breath too long
exercise: Why does the glass move on a spirit board? | Tiny movements people make without knowing it | Spirits of the dead push it along to spell out words | The board has a strong magnet hidden underneath it | A draught of wind blows through the room and moves it
exercise: What is the ideomotor effect? | Tiny movements people make without knowing it | A ghost moving objects about in a dark, quiet room | A kind of tiny motor hidden inside a toy or a game | A magic spell for seeing clearly in the dark at night
exercise: Are black cats bad luck? | No, it is a superstition, and some places say they are lucky | Yes, everywhere in the world | Only if they cross from left to right | Only on Friday the 13th
exercise: What is the "calls from inside the house" story? | An old urban legend that was made into films | A true story taken straight from the police files | A news report from a local paper only last week | A rule about when you may use the house phone
exercise: Why do shapes and sounds feel scarier in the dark? | Our brains are more likely to imagine danger | Ghosts only come out at night | Our ears get bigger at night | Things really do change their shape in the dark
exercise: What should you do if someone at a sleepover is truly scared? | Stop the story and remind them it's a game | Make the story even scarier so it's more fun | Laugh about it and tell all the others at school | Turn off all the lights so the story feels real
exercise: What is something under the bed at midnight most likely to be? | Your imagination, in the dark | A monster that grabs ankles | A ghost that lives there | A burglar who hides every night
exercise: What should you do when there's a spooky noise you can't explain? | Turn on a light, look, or tell an adult | Hide right under the duvet until it's morning | Call the ghost hunters from the TV show to come | Shout loudly at the noise to make it go away
test: "Bloody Mary bloody mary bloody mary... SHE'S THERE I SAW HER FACE CHANGE" What really happened? | Staring in dim light makes your brain warp your face | Bloody Mary appeared because they said it three times | The mirror was haunted by a girl | Someone was standing behind her
test: An older kid tells the hook man story as if it happened in your town. What's the clue it's a legend? | It's been told in many places since the 1950s | It has a man in it | It happened at night | It's about a car
test: Your little sister hears scratching and screams "GHOST!" What's a kind, true answer? | It's probably mice or a branch; let's check with a light | Yes, it's a ghost, hide under your duvet | Scratching on the wall means a ghost wants to come in now | Ignore her, she's being a baby
test: "if u fall asleep first we're putting ur hand in warm water lol" What's fair to say? | There's no good evidence it works, and it's mean to prank sleepers | It works every time, so stay awake | Only cold water works, not warm | It's fine because everyone does it
test: At camp, a leader tells kids a monster lives in the lake. What would happen if kids swam there alone at night? | They could be in real danger from cold, deep water | They would be pulled under the water by the lake monster | Nothing, lakes are always safe at night | The monster would chase them out
test: The glass slides across the spirit board and spells a name. Is a spirit moving it? | No, tiny unnoticed movements of people's fingers move it | Yes, that's how spirits talk to us | Only if the lights are off | Only if everyone is very quiet
test: "black cat crossed my path, my test is gonna go terrible" What do you say? | It's a superstition; some places even say they're lucky | You'd better skip the test now | Walk backwards three times round the house to undo the bad luck | Only a white cat can cancel it
test: "the babysitter got calls from INSIDE THE HOUSE, it's true it was in a film" What's true? | It's an old urban legend that was made into films | It's true because films show real events | It happened to a babysitter in your town just last year | The police confirmed it last week
test: Your cousin says something grabs your ankle if you look under the bed at midnight. What would really happen? | Nothing; you'd see dust and maybe a lost sock | A cold bony hand would reach out and grab your arm | The whole bed would start to shake and float up | You'd fall into a deep sleep and never wake up
test: Everyone at the sleepover is laughing, but one friend is shaking and wants to go home. What do you do? | Stop the scary stories and help them feel safe | Tell them another scary story to toughen them up | Laugh so they see it's silly | Leave them alone so they calm down
exam: Identify the phenomenon by which a pointer upon a spirit board is caused to move. | The ideomotor effect, consisting of involuntary muscular movements | The intervention of the spirits of the deceased | The operation of a concealed magnet | The action of a draught of air
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the alteration of one's reflected face observed in dim light? | It arises from the processing of the image within the brain | It is caused by a spirit within the mirror | It results from the bending of light by mirrors of great age | It occurs solely upon the recitation of a name
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the danger presented by a lake to an unaccompanied child. | Deep water may be cold, deep and obstructed by weeds | A lake presents no danger in the absence of a monster | Lakes are dangerous solely at midnight | Only shallow water presents any danger
exam: It is given that scratching sounds are audible within a house at night. Determine the most probable explanation. | Animals, vegetation, wind, pipework or the settling of the building | The presence of a ghost seeking admission | A monster resident within the walls | The house's preparation for an earthquake
